Let T:U\to V and S:V\to W be linear maps. Denote A = \mathcal {M}(S) and C = \mathcal {M}(T). Then the composition of linear maps ST is computed
\begin {align*} (ST)(u)_k &= S(\sum _{r=1}^n C_{rk}v_r) \\ &= \sum _{r=1}^n C_{rk}S(v_r) \\ &= \sum _{r=1}^n C_{rk}\sum _{s=1}^m A _{sr}w_s \\ &= \sum _{s=1}^m\left (\sum _{r=1}^n C_{rk}A_{sr}\right )w_s \\ \end {align*}
Thus \mathcal {M}(ST) is the m\times p whose entries are
\mathcal {M}(ST)_{sk} = \sum _{r=1}^n A_{sr}C_{rk}

fr:mainmatter.html:p:
Suppose A is an m\times n matrix and B is an n\times p matrix. Then the entry of the product AB is:
(AB)_{ij} = A_{i\cdot }B_{\cdot j}
for 1\leq i\leq m and 1\leq j\leq p.

Let A be an m\times n matrix, and c is a 1\times 1 matrix.
c = \begin {pmatrix} c_1 \\ c_2 \\ \vdots \\ c_n \end {pmatrix}
Then Ac = c_1A_{\cdot 1} + c_2A_{\cdot 2} + \cdots + c_nA_{\cdot n}. In other words, Ac is a linear Combination of the columns of A, with the scalars that multiply the columns coming from c.

A linear map T\in \mathcal {L}(V,W) is said to be invertible if there exists a linear map S\in \mathcal {L}(W,V) such that:
\begin {align*} T\cdot S &= \text {id}_V \\ S\cdot T &= \text {id}_W \end {align*}
where \text {id} is the identity map. If a linear map T is invertible, then the map S is unique and is called the inverse of T, denoted T^{-1}.

